LOGBOOK.md:786:- FINDING (review round 2, blocking): seven fault boundaries were pinned at 2204, but only three carried a recovery half. Staged mode drift, both FIFO shard cases and BOTH quarantine-ENOSPC subtests asserted the failure state and stopped. Anything can fail; what a durability suite exists to establish is what a later run can still do. Both quarantine-ENOSPC cases are full-disk cases, so this was a direct miss against the front-loaded rule that full-disk and short-write cases must show the store is left recoverable.
LOGBOOK.md:789:- DECISION: the two quarantine-ENOSPC recoveries have a different SHAPE and the tests now say so at the case. A candidate that was never a valid object cannot install on retry, so its recovery is that it is preserved as evidence exactly as it would have been without the fault. A corrupt existing object is deliberately left at the digest path, so its recovery is what the NEXT run does with it still there.
LOGBOOK.md:791:- EVIDENCE: 5 mutants, 5 killed, transcript in `TASK-260830-3qrfjp_recovery-mutation-evidence.log`. The three that matter each leave the FAULTED first half of the case passing and die only at a line added in this round. M5 moves the existing artifact but never reports its path (`object_store_fault_test.go:490`). M6 quarantines the mismatched candidate but never reports its path (`:434`). M4 classifies a genuine ABSENCE as an unsafe entry and is killed by all six retry halves at `:247`, `:291`, `:366` (both subtests), and `object_store_fault_unix_test.go:109`, `:147` — the absence-versus-failure-to-read distinction, seen from the recovery side.
LOGBOOK.md:792:- NOTE: the `assertQuarantineIsEmpty` added to the group-accessible-shard case is a state assertion backing a README claim, not a gate proof; that refusal returns before anything is staged, so no natural mutant can put something in quarantine there. Recorded rather than presented as mutation-tested.
LOGBOOK.md:799:- FINDING: on a delayed-allocation filesystem (ext4, XFS, APFS) ENOSPC surfaces at writeback, not at the accepting write. So the disagreement is reachable with NO error raised on either side: `io.Copy` reports the full length, the hasher agrees with the declared digest, and only `fstat` disagrees.
LOGBOOK.md:802:- DECISION: write-side faults are driven through the PRE-EXISTING `storeOperations.syncFile` seam, not a new one. fsync is where a real ENOSPC is discovered, so a failure raised there and a length that changed by stat time are the two shapes the condition actually takes. No new production seam was added to the object store.
